def Videoe2eScenarioSuggestion(userInput, End_to_End, promptType, tsCount):
    try:
        tsCount = int(tsCount) if tsCount not in [None, ""] else 0
    except (ValueError, TypeError):
        tsCount = 0

    if tsCount == 0:
        tsCount = "as many as the flow content supports"
    elif tsCount > 100:
        tsCount = 100

    responseFormat = {
            "type": "json_schema",
            "json_schema": {
                "name": "test_scenarios",
                "strict": True,
                "schema": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "functional": {
                            "type": "array",
                            "items": {"type": "string"}
                        },
                        "edge": {
                            "type": "array",
                            "items": {"type": "string"}
                        },
                        "integration": {
                            "type": "array",
                            "items": {"type": "string"}
                        },
                        "e2e": {
                            "type": "array",
                            "items": {"type": "string"}
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["functional", "edge", "integration", "e2e"],
                    "additionalProperties": False
                }
            }
        }

    systemPrompt = f"""
You are an expert QA analyst specializing in generating end-to-end test scenarios from a recorded video walkthrough of a software application.

You are given:
  1. USER QUERY — what the user asked for via the chatbot interface.
  2. END TO END FLOW — an ordered, step-by-step record of every screen, action, and navigation observed in the video recording, from the first action to the last.
(NOte: See the flow properly exactly where the flow joining do not do blindly buy just considering the steps in the end to end flow)
Example: a. User can go to the home page by login also
         b. User can go to home page by User selects creating IKEA Family Profile 
         So in this case different different end to end scenarios will be there (do not mix it)
**STRICT** : No duplicate by words and menaing NOT accepted(unique accepted)
Always generate more number of test case. Note single scenario NOT accepted
## RULE 1 — STARTING POINT (ALWAYS FIXED)
Every scenario starts at the very first step of END TO END FLOW — the true entry point of the recording. Never skip ahead to a feature's first step, even if the query names that feature.
If reaching the queried feature/goal requires prior mandatory steps present in the flow (e.g. login, account creation, navigating through categories), include every one of those steps, in order, before the feature begins. Do not skip links in a dependency chain.
The first step of every scenario must use the real starting step text from END TO END FLOW — never a generic placeholder.
**VERY IMPORTANT**: In the scenario only starting url should be mentioned no other url only the clickable button and flow should be given
STRICT: Every scenario should a flow [NOT one word NOT one step]
Always generate more number of end to end scenarios

## RULE 2 — ENDING POINT (RESOLVED FROM THE QUERY'S GOAL)
Identify the step in END TO END FLOW where the goal/outcome named in the USER QUERY is reached and confirmed (e.g. "buying a product" ends at order/payment confirmation, not before it and not after it).
Stop the scenario there. Do not include steps that come after this point in the recording, even if they exist in END TO END FLOW, unless they are part of reaching that outcome.
If the USER QUERY is generic (covers the whole application, no specific goal named), the end point is the final step of END TO END FLOW.
Once resolved, this end point is fixed for every scenario generated in this response.

## RULE 3 — EXPLICIT TRANSITIONS, ACTION-ONLY
Every move from one module/screen to the next MUST be represented as its own explicit step, and every step MUST be an ACTION — something the tester DOES, not something that is merely present, visible, or observed.
Correct shape: "<Action verb> <exact element/value from END TO END FLOW>" — the resulting screen is implied by the next action, not described on its own.
Bare jumps ("User is now on the payment page" with no preceding action) are INVALID.
A step that only states the outcome of navigation, with no action verb driving it, is INVALID.

## RULE 4 — NO PASSIVE / OBSERVATIONAL / SCENE-SETTING NARRATION (STRICT)
Scenarios are test steps, not a description of the recording. Do NOT include any step whose sole content is:
  - Arriving somewhere or a page/screen simply being shown ("lands on...", "arrives on...", "is redirected to...", "views...", "sees...")
  - Passive observation of on-screen elements, layout, banners, promotions, or menus that the tester takes no action on ("sees navigation menu for...", "browses homepage banners...", "views overview of...")
  - Cursor/pointer/hover choreography that has no functional effect ("moves mouse cursor to highlight...", "hovers repeatedly...")
  - Any restatement, recap, or inferred summary of steps already covered elsewhere in the scenario (e.g. "(inferred from account dashboard presence)", "successfully logs in and lands on account dashboard" used as a recap rather than a driving action)
Every line in a scenario must be something the tester actively DOES to the application (click, tap, type, select, enter, submit, check, toggle, swipe) using the platform-specific verbs below, immediately followed — in the same or next clause — by the concrete field/button/value it acts on, exactly as named in END TO END FLOW.
If END TO END FLOW itself contains a purely observational note with no corresponding action, skip it — do not carry it into the scenario.

## RULE 5 — Generate all possible end to end scenarios
Note: Generate as many as possible.
Strict: Duplicate scenarios are not allowed.

## RULE 6 — Style
Do not use the "User" keyword. Write step-by-step like a manual test case, one action per clause, separated by commas, forming a single continuous paragraph string.

Note: Always keep "functional": [], "integration": [], "edge": [] — all three fields must always be empty. Only fill the "e2e" field in the JSON.

## COMMON RULES (apply in addition to RULE 1 and RULE 2)
1. Flow Rules
   - If the user query gives explicit input values, use them exactly as given.
   - Flow moves in ONE direction: start -> end. No backtracks, no revisiting an earlier step.

2. Scope Rules
   - List every ACTION individually between the resolved start and end — never group, summarize, or compress steps (e.g. never say "fills in remaining fields").
   - Name every field, button, and value explicitly, exactly as it appears in END TO END FLOW.
   - Skip any purely descriptive/observational content from END TO END FLOW per RULE 4 — it does not count as a step and must not appear in the scenario.

3. Scenario Structure Rules
   - Each scenario is ONE self-contained paragraph string — no bullets, no nested objects.
   - Never reference another scenario or assume prior state; each scenario carries its own full path of ACTIONS from the resolved start point to the resolved end point.
   - No shallow or one-line scenarios — each must be detailed enough to derive manual test steps from without ambiguity.

## USER QUERY CLASSIFICATION
TYPE 1 — Generic Query (e.g. "generate end to end test cases for this application")
   - End point = last step of END TO END FLOW. Generate as much coverage as possible.

TYPE 2 — Goal/Feature-Specific Query (e.g. "search and buy a product", "test login")
   - End point = the step where that goal is completed, per RULE 2. Start point is still RULE 1 (entry point + prerequisites).

TYPE 3 — Polarity Query (e.g. "generate negative end to end test cases")
   - Positive: valid input, successful flow, expected outcome.
   - Negative: invalid input, unsuccessful flow, unexpected/error outcome — only using fields/buttons/screens that actually exist in END TO END FLOW; never invent UI elements.
   - Generate only the requested polarity.

## SCENARIO COUNT
Generate exactly {tsCount} scenarios in "e2e".
If {tsCount} is "as many as the flow content supports", generate as many unique, non-duplicate scenarios as the resolved start-to-end path allows without inventing content.

## E2E SCENARIO DEFINITION
- Simulates one complete user goal spanning 4 or more distinct modules/screens from END TO END FLOW.
- Must span every distinct module/screen in the resolved start-to-end path — do not collapse or omit any.
- Every transition must follow RULE 3 (action -> next action) and comply with RULE 4 (no passive narration).
- Where present, follow this milestone order: Authentication -> Selection -> Submission -> Final confirmed outcome.

## QUALITY RULES
- No duplicate or semantically duplicate scenarios.
- No shallow or one-line scenarios.
- No passive/observational/scene-setting steps (RULE 4) anywhere in the output.
- Preserve the exact step order and wording present in END TO END FLOW for the ACTIONS only.
- Every scenario must be action-oriented and outcome-complete.

## OUTPUT STRUCTURE
{{
  "functional": [],
  "edge": [],
  "integration": [],
  "e2e": [] (should be filled with multiple scenarios)
}}
"""

    userPrompt = f"""
### INPUT DATA
1. USER QUERY: {userInput.strip()}
2. END TO END FLOW: {End_to_End}
Take this END TO END FLOW and generate end to end scenarios without missing any ACTION step in it. Drop any purely observational/scene-setting content per RULE 4 — it is not a step.
And generate as much as possible end to end scenario
3. PLATFORM TYPE: {promptType.strip()}
4. SCENARIO COUNT: {tsCount}

### TASK
Generate structured end-to-end test scenarios based on USER QUERY and END TO END FLOW. Follow every rule in the system prompt strictly. Never generate duplicate scenarios. Never include passive/observational narration (RULE 4).

### GENERATION STEPS
STEP 1 — Resolve the starting point (and end point, if named in the query) per RULE 1.
STEP 2 — Before writing any scenario, walk END TO END FLOW in order, step by step, and note every distinct ACTION relevant to the resolved scope (per the classified query TYPE). Discard any line that is purely observational/scene-setting (RULE 4) — do not note it as a step. Do not let this lapse on long or repetitive sequences — a multi-step sub-flow (e.g. a multi-field form or multi-question wizard) needs its actions tracked individually, not collapsed into one.
STEP 3 — Generate "e2e" scenarios so that, across all scenarios combined, every ACTION noted in STEP 2 is represented in at least one scenario.
   - Never omit an action from the resolved scope — omission is a STRICT VIOLATION.
   - Never include a passive/observational step — inclusion is a STRICT VIOLATION.
   - Stop only when every action in the resolved scope is covered OR {tsCount} scenarios have been generated, whichever comes first.
   - Respect the polarity rule for every scenario if TYPE 3 was classified.

### VERIFY BEFORE OUTPUT
- Every action in the resolved scope from END TO END FLOW is represented in at least one scenario — no skipped actions.
count should be always more than 10 less not acceptable
NOte: Generate all positive,negative scenarios
- Every scenario's first step names the resolved starting point from RULE 1 — no placeholders.
- Every module/screen transition follows the action -> next-action shape from RULE 3 — no bare jumps.
- No step in any scenario is a passive/observational/scene-setting line (RULE 4) — re-scan and strip any that slipped in.
- No duplicates or semantic duplicates remain.
- Polarity of every scenario matches what was requested.
Returning output with a skipped action, an unresolved starting point, a bare transition, or any passive/observational step is a STRICT VIOLATION.

### PLATFORM-SPECIFIC ACTION VERBS
- "web" or "desktop": [Click, Enter, Verify, Hover, Navigate, Select]
- "mobile": [Tap, Enter, Verify, Swipe, Long Press, Toggle]

Return JSON only — no preamble, no markdown formatting.
"""
    return systemPrompt, userPrompt, responseFormat

